Juvenile idiopathic arthritis
Paediatric & Neonatal
Chronic joint swelling > 6 weeks, Morning stiffness improving with activity, Age < 16 years

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-027
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe joint pain and inability to bear weight in a child, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out septic arthritis or acute osteomyelitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a child refusing to walk, presenting with a swollen, warm knee. Immediate assessment was mandated to rule out an acute, joint-destroying bacterial infection (septic arthritis) prior to the next in-hours period, which could cause irreversible cartilage necrosis if delayed.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Her knee is huge and warm, and she completely refused to walk on it when she woke up this morning."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a persistent, chronic joint swelling explicitly lasting for eight continuous weeks, which acutely flared over the past 24 hours. The chronological timeline features mild, intermittent limp developing two months ago, progressing to pronounced limping, and culminating in a total refusal to walk immediately upon awakening today. The patient describes severe morning stiffness that reliably improves with physical activity as the day progresses. The patient is 9 years old. The parents note a low-grade, quotidian fever spiking strictly once daily over the last week, occasionally accompanied by a faint, transient salmon-pink macular rash.
Functional Status: Currently limping heavily, entirely reliant on caregivers for mobility in the morning.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired mobility restricting school attendance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.4C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 30 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the right knee, no acute toxic appearance.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination reveals marked, asymmetric effusion of the right knee. There is prominent warmth and restricted active and passive range of motion strictly localized to the affected joint. There is an absolute absence of overlying skin erythema. The left knee and bilateral ankles are unaffected. The diagnostic tetrad of chronic joint swelling > 6 weeks, morning stiffness improving with activity, and age < 16 years is confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Bedside commode temporarily arranged by parents due to poor morning mobility.
Medication Reconciliation: Intermittent ibuprofen yielding mild, temporary relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (Oligoarticular subtype) - Status: Acute flare
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out by the chronicity of the swelling (8 weeks), the lack of exquisite, screaming pain on passive micro-motion, the absence of high continuous fever, and the lack of overlying fiery erythema.

Transient Synovitis - Ruled out explicitly by the prolonged duration (greater than 6 weeks), as transient synovitis self-resolves within 1 to 2 weeks.

Osteomyelitis - Ruled out by the lack of focal bony point tenderness adjacent to the joint and the absence of a toxic, septic presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk of chronic joint contracture)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of an acutely painful, swollen, and warm joint, accompanied by an inability to bear weight, necessitated urgent after-hours assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified the critical need to exclude septic arthritis, an orthopedic emergency. Objective clinical evaluation confirmed a chronic inflammatory effusion characteristic of Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis, explicitly ruling out a rapidly destructive bacterial joint infection via the absence of extreme pain on micro-motion and lack of overlying erythema. Delaying this assessment would have left the patient in severe pain and the parents in extreme distress regarding a potential bone infection. Immediate intervention provided robust systemic analgesia and initiated the definitive rheumatological diagnostic pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed strict, scheduled, weight-based non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (Naproxen 10mg/kg BD) for sustained anti-inflammatory effect. Generated an urgent referral to pediatric rheumatology and ophthalmology.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instruct the patient to perform gentle, non-weight-bearing joint range-of-motion exercises (like riding a bicycle in the air) while still lying in bed for 10 minutes immediately upon waking. By rewarding this specific, low-impact movement with a warm heat pack directly afterward, the patient actively mechanically lubricates the joint, physically breaking the severe morning stiffness and facilitating weight-bearing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provide a warm compress for morning application.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to order baseline inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP, ANA).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the joint suddenly becomes bright red, exquisitely agonizing to touch, or if the patient develops a continuous high fever with severe chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Final diagnosis requires formal rheumatological evaluation and serological testing.
Clinical Course: The inflammation will persist but should become manageable with scheduled daily NSAIDs. Chronic untreated inflammation risks permanent joint damage and asymptomatic eye inflammation (uveitis).

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the skin over the knee turns bright red and hot, or if the child develops a high fever that does not come down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The child's immune system is chronically irritating the lining of the joint, causing fluid build-up and severe stiffness, particularly after resting. It is not a bacterial infection. The daily medication must be taken even on days when the pain is mild to constantly suppress this immune reaction.
Actionable Steps: Apply heat packs directly to the knee in the morning. Ensure the child attends the urgent eye doctor appointment, as this arthritis can cause silent damage to the eyes without any visible redness.

